{"name":"Paper White","hex":"#f6efdf","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"paper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sheet material typically used for writing on or printing on (or as a non-waterproof container), usually made by draining cellulose fibres from a suspension in water.","Ellipsis of newspaper; anything used as such (such as a newsletter or listing magazine)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of paper.","Insubstantial (from the weakness of common paper)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/paper"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Paper","blocks":[{"text":"Paper is a thin sheet of matted cellulose fibers. Largely derived from lignocellulose, paper is created from a pulp dissolved into a slurry that is drained and dried into sheets. Different types of paper are defined by constituent fiber, paper pulp, sizing, coating, paper size, paper density and grammage."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG/330px-Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paper","wikidata":"Q11472"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG/500px-Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG/1280px-Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG/500px-Various_products_made_from_paper.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVarious_products_made_from_paper.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Chinese_hemp_paper_western_han.jpg/500px-Chinese_hemp_paper_western_han.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Chinese_hemp_paper_western_han.jpg/500px-Chinese_hemp_paper_western_han.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hemp wrapping paper, China, c. 100 BCE","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChinese_hemp_paper_western_han.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/PaperAutofluorescence.jpg/500px-PaperAutofluorescence.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/PaperAutofluore